Follow-up data were available for eighteen (94.7%) patients, and the
median duration after immunotherapy was three months (range,
1–13). Clinical outcomes were measured by the mRS, and improvement
was de ﬁned as a decrease in the mRS score of one or more from the
baseline score. Among fourteen patients who received immunotherapy,
eight (57.1%) patients showed clinical improvement. Immunotherapy
generally showed favorable responses, with mRS scores falling from a
median of 2 at baseline to 1 ( Fig. 3A, P = 0.012). None of the four pa-
tients who did not receive immunotherapy showed clinical improve-
ment. According to neurological syndromes, limbic encephalitis
demonstrated a good response to immunotherapy; all four patients
showed improvement. Furthermore, marked improvement, with mRS
scores falling more than one point, occurred only in the limbic enceph-
alitis subgroup (Fig. 3B). Among three patients with epilepsy, only one
received immunotherapy and improved. In the cerebellar ataxia sub-
group, one out of three patients experienced improvement. One patient
with stiff-person syndrome did not respond to treatment. In the others
group, two patients (40%) showed improvement: one with orthostatic
dizziness and the other with diplopia. Regarding immunotherapy,
additional 2nd line immunotherapy or long-term maintenance therapy
